  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WASH in Schools III</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1260 students (Three public schools with an average of 420 students per school).</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon - areas that host Syrian refugees.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Lebanon is experiencing an unprecedented protracted crisis that strained the country&#x2019;s resilience and crippled the country&#x2019;s ability to provide basic services. Lebanese schools&apos; capacity is overstretched, as they are providing education to both local population and refugees. Often in two shifts per day. Drop-out rates are at risk of rising, due to insufficient water and sanitation in the schools to provide a clean and safe environment for girls and boys. This project aim is to strengthen the community&#x2019;s resilience and prevent waterborne diseases.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to Lebanese schools that host both children of Syrian refugee population and host communities. The project supports the water and sanitation structures in the schools, as well as promotion of hygiene practices among the pupils. With the help of the WASH in Schools II project, the LRC WASH team can thus promote safer hygiene practices, which is especially important in light of the COVID-19 pandemic.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aim is to improve water and sanitation infrastructure and hygiene practices to reduce exposure of vulnerable communities to WASH-related diseases, increase their resilience, and preserve their dignity through environmentally sustainable approaches. 

This project will achieve the strategic objective through the following outcomes and outputs:
Outcome 1: Institutions have improved access to safe water, sanitation facilities, and hygiene practices through environmentally sustainable infrastructure and participatory approaches
Output 1.1: Institutions water systems are rehabilitated to provide safe water supply
Output 1.2: Water networks linking water sources to institutions provide safe water supply
Output 1.3: Environmentally sustainable infrastructure is installed for sufficient operational systems
Output 1.4: Individuals in institutions have access to safe systems that ensure hygienic disposal and management of excreta
Output 1.5: Institutions are safe from accumulation of stagnant storm water
Output 1.6: Students are utilizing the appropriate hygiene items effectively</narrative>
  </description>
